WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.480
This episode is sponsored by Wren, a website
where you calculate your carbon footprint.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.800
You can also sign up to make a monthly contribution
to offset your carbon footprint or support

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.469
rainforest protection projects.

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:36.050
So at the end of this episode, I’m going
to say the thing that I usually say at the

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.600
end of the episodes, that thing where I thank
you all for coming on this journey with us

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.950
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds
us.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:47.059
But in the minutes we have before we get there,
let’s acknowledge something: most of the

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:52.910
time, the world that we are talking about
has come from trees and ponds and beach sand.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:56.980
You know, the great outdoors, the beauty of
nature.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.410
But you know what’s also great?

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.410
The indoors.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.489
It’s comfy and cozy, and it’s not just
great for you.

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:07.030
Every time you go out, whether it’s to run
some errands or relax at the park, you bring

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.850
some friends back with you.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:14.159
And when you wash your hands, some of those
friends will end up overstaying their welcome—in

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:18.890
your sink.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.580
Here at Journey to the Microcosmos, we’ve
been wanting to explore the world inside our

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.900
drains for a while.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.700
But it’s one thing to know what’s inside
a sink, it’s a whole different thing to

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.520
know what’s inside your sink.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.509
So understandably, James—our master of microscopes—was
a little reluctant to delve into the contents

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:41.560
of his own home’s pipes and then share it
with the entire internet.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:48.399
So instead, he used a popular vacation rental
app and rented the house next door and checked

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:49.479
their pipes instead.

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:54.540
That’s right, he went to a vacation rental,
and opened up the u-bend pipe under the bathroom

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.479
sink to gather up some of the hideous slime
he found inside.

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:02.479
It might help a bit to understand the u-bend
of a pipe.

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:07.689
One of the things you don’t want is for
your pipe to be an open-air line to the sewer...if

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:12.349
that were the case, you’d be able to smell
the sewer from your home.

00:02:12.349 --> 00:02:17.090
So every drain you’ve got has a little u-shaped
bend somewhere in it and that u-bend is always

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:22.430
full of water, that prevents sewer gasses
from invading your home.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.450
But a place that is always wet is going to
be full of microbes.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:33.290
James has been collecting samples every day
for years, turning to all sorts of ingenious

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.940
methods in uncomfortable situations to gather
what he needs.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.870
But he said that this was the first time he
did not enjoy the process.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.540
So thank you, James, for your service.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.650
We do deeply appreciate it.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:50.490
By the way, now would be a good time to remind
you that James’s book “The Hidden Beauty

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.330
of the Microscopic World” is available wherever
you get your books.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:59.300
It lets you hold the microcosmos in your hands
with page after page of beautiful pictures

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.650
and information.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.409
But, back to our drain samples.

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:06.640
The slime that James was collecting from is
called biofilm.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.040
You might find it in your drains or on other
surfaces, say a sink that hasn’t been cleaned

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.220
in a while.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:17.490
And while it’s gross to look at from our
perspective, it’s actually kind of endearing

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.650
when you think about it from the microbe’s
point of view.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.489
Biofilm is a sort of a community venture,
bringing together microbes that stick together

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:28.700
for their collective survival.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:34.319
In our case, you can see the long hyphae of
fungi weaving together through the film, creating

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:39.069
an architecture that other non motile prokaryotes
can cling to.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:45.230
Also, give us a moment to show off what we
think might be a first on our channel: archaea.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.120
It’s not that we haven’t been able to
see archaea before.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.879
But archaea and bacteria are both prokaryotes,
and with our previous microscopic techniques,

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:56.080
it’s been difficult to distinguish between
the two.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.880
But archaea are an entirely different kingdom
of life than bacteria.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.130
Archaea are likely more primitive, and they
have different chemistries in their cell wall

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.239
and their metabolism.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:13.049
With our new fluorescence light illumination,
we can see some autofluorescence emitting

00:04:13.049 --> 00:04:14.180
from these samples.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:20.889
And we think those bits of autofluorescence
are archaea, glowing blue under the UV light.

00:04:20.889 --> 00:04:25.780
There are other beautiful bits of autofluorescence
that have come from this gross slime as well,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.200
like this mysterious illuminated organism.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:35.820
But no matter how beautiful it is, let’s
not forget where these samples came from: a drain.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.600
And drains are exciting for most of us.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.400
They’re a tool, they are a fixture, a means
to maintain our environment.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:48.530
They keep water contained and traveling (hopefully)
in one direction: away from us.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.920
But for these organisms, drains are their
environment.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.490
They’re dark, and damp, and mostly protected,
though they do go through periodic flooding

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.789
that brings new nutrients and companions.

00:05:00.789 --> 00:05:06.590
Forming a biofilm helps the organisms contend
with whatever we throw their way, including

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:11.190
making the microbes inside them better able
to resist disinfectants.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:15.870
Biofilms may be gross and inconvenient for
us, but they provide microbes more stability

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:19.500
than if they were free-floating in their planktonic
forms.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.120
But there are limits to the protection a biofilm
can offer.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.250
There are protist predators that are quite
content to munch on this community, which

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:32.530
is bad news for the organisms inside the biofilm,
but great news for the protists that eat them.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:38.979
It’s also great news for more complex organisms,
like nematodes and rotifers, that need those

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:44.390
protists to build out the food chain that
will ultimately lead to their own dinner.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:48.730
And if that food proves to be plentiful, then
they might contentedly and serenely sift through

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:52.340
clouds of bacteria like this rotifer.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:56.870
Since we’re looking through the microscope
and not directly at the drain itself, we don’t

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.190
know what this individual was doing when it
was living in sink scum.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:07.240
But proabbly this was its life back when it
was living tucked away in the u-bend of a

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.240
stranger’s house.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:12.419
If you’ve been watching this channel for
a while, you might find that scene familiar,

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:14.630
maybe surprisingly so.

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:20.259
Maybe you were expecting or hoping for swamp
monsters in the slime, something completely

00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:23.690
wild and different from other things that
we see under the microscope.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:27.160
But the familiarity is kind of the point.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:31.990
These things in our sinks are there because
we put them there, bringing them from the

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.530
surfaces around us.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:40.380
It’s an ecosystem of our own making, structured
by our architecture and fed by our own daily

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.030
adventures and misadventures.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:48.479
This house’s drain is like a little history
of the people who have stayed there before,

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:51.449
a microbial guest book, if you will.

00:06:51.449 --> 00:06:55.750
And that extends to all sorts of drains, not
just the ones in our homes.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:07:00.170
After all, there are drains in buildings and
facilities where we work and eat and visit.

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:06.699
And they all have their own little ecosystems
fed by whatever is going on in that building.

00:07:06.699 --> 00:07:08.930
Sometimes those ecosystems are innocuous.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:12.720
In food processing plants that make cheese,
for instance, scientists have found the microbes

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.150
used to make cheese lingering in the those
drains.

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.970
But sometimes the ecosystems have their own
monsters, like the pathogenic bacteria found

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:22.310
in hospital drains.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.680
We might be studying this house’s drains
for fun and education.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.210
But for hospitals, those drains are both a
record and indicator of disease, making them

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:35.480
a concern for public health while also providing
us with the means to understand what those

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.720
concerns might need to be.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.470
Because of course, no ecology exists on its
own.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:48.090
The drain isn’t just the unseen world around
us, it’s an unseen world that we make and

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:53.979
interact with all the time, in stories of
causes and effects that seem distant from

00:07:53.979 --> 00:07:56.370
the creatures idling inside.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:00.280
It may not be born in the most elegant of
ways, and it may never be beautiful to look

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:01.610
at from afar.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:08.300
But up close, even a drain can be breathtaking.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:14.590
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

